<div class="note">
<strong>Why mono-colored cards beat dual-colored lands at the top.</strong> A colorless card or a single-color card fits more decks than a two-color card. A black-only card fits every mono-black deck plus every Rakdos (black-red) deck plus every Orzhov (white-black) deck plus every Mardu (white-black-red) deck, that's 4 of the 17 we're tracking. A black-red land fits only 9 of 25 because it can't go in any mono deck or any white-only / red-only / black-only deck. This is just a math fact about how many decks each card is "legal" in, not a claim that the card is automatically played in every deck of its color.
</div>
<div class="note warn">
<strong>How to read this in practice.</strong> Pure "deck count" rewards cards that fit anywhere, especially colorless artifacts. But every two-color or three-color deck still needs special two-color "dual lands" so that you can tap for both colors on the same turn. Your dual-land collection is already deep, 19 of the 22 most-played Mardu-color duals are at full playset. The single biggest hole is the three "shock lands" (Blood Crypt for black-red, Godless Shrine for white-black, Sacred Foundry for white-red). You own zero of all three, and they're the lands that enter the battlefield untapped on demand, which is what makes a three-color manabase work. Even though their deck-count score is lower than mono-black mythics, they fix a problem the rest of your collection cannot.
</div>

<h2 id="archetypes">The 17 decks the ranking uses for scoring</h2>
<p class="sub">"Mardu" is a shorthand for "white, black, and red, in any combination", a three-color slice of Magic's five colors. The grid below is every deck in that slice currently being played on Arena, pulled live from Untapped.gg over the last 30 days. Mythic-tier winrate is the percentage of games the deck wins when played by mythic-ranked players. Decks in red text have a winrate above 55% with at least 100 sampled matches. <strong>Mardu Discard at 60.1% winrate is the strongest Mardu-color deck in the format right now.</strong> The card-ranking above counts how many of these 17 decks can legally include each card.</p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Is it stuck at 17 decks?</strong> No. The 17 is not a cap, it is every Mardu-color archetype that cleared a minimum-sample threshold (at least 30 mythic-tier matches) in the live Untapped meta period at build time. Lower that threshold and you get more, lower-sample decks; raise it and you get fewer, more-proven ones. The data was pulled by hand from Untapped, so the page does not yet refresh on its own. A once-a-day auto-rebuild is straightforward to wire up, see the note at the bottom of the page for the three update options.
</div>
